		{"id":"d1e2c3b4a5f60001","type":"text","text":"## Stack and Platform Decisions\n\n- **Tauri v2** (not v1) - project policy: keep Tauri and Angular on the latest stable version at all times, never pin once and forget. Reduces attack surface (outdated frameworks accumulate vulnerabilities).\n- **Rust is the source of truth for state** - the presentation layer (Angular today) is replaceable (e.g. by Flutter) without touching the core; state on the presentation side is only a projection/cache, never a second source of truth. Tauri's Commands/Events bridge is also treated as replaceable, not an assumption of the core.\n- **Angular with only native framework features** - no third-party npm packages, to minimize supply-chain attack surface.\n- **State management: Signals** (native to Angular) - not NgRx, not pure RxJS as the primary state source.","x":-320,"y":700,"width":650,"height":460},
		{"id":"d1e2c3b4a5f60002","type":"text","text":"## Rust Core Decisions\n\n- **Code familiar to people coming from TypeScript** (Angular/Nest.js), not advanced idiomatic Rust: each domain module is a struct with a constructor receiving dependencies explicitly (roughly Nest.js's @Injectable(), with no DI container); Session Manager is a thin router (roughly a Nest.js controller) - it only dispatches, it doesn't accumulate logic.\n- **Concurrency model: as simple as possible** - a single Arc<Mutex<AppState>> (or one Mutex per module, no more than that). A Command handler locks/changes/releases. The cpal real-time thread never touches this Mutex - it only reads already-prepared atomics/ring buffers. Only add complexity (channels, actor, granular mutex) if profiling shows real contention - the target is one person clicking buttons, not a concurrent server.\n- **Audio crates, cross-platform (Linux/Windows/macOS/Android):** symphonia (decoding, pure Rust), rtrb (SPSC ring buffer between decoding and the real-time callback), cpal (audio output - ALSA/WASAPI/CoreAudio/Oboe). Android via Oboe needs the JVM context that Tauri mobile exposes.\n- **Loop boundary: short crossfade** (a few ms), not a hard cut - avoids a click if the marker doesn't land on a zero-crossing. Requires the start of the loop pre-loaded in a separate buffer before the callback reaches the end.\n- **Stem channels: always stereo** - a mono stem is upmixed (channel duplicated L/R) at import time, not on every playback. Different from sample rate (which is rejected, not converted) because duplicating mono has no quality ambiguity.","x":380,"y":700,"width":750,"height":640},
		{"id":"d1e2c3b4a5f60003","type":"text","text":"## Persistence Decisions\n\n- **Every disk write is atomic**, no exceptions - write-to-temp-file + rename (the project's .json, the waveform cache, and in the future the .cho). rename is atomic at the file-system level: it never corrupts the previous file if the app crashes mid-write.\n- **Waveform cache on disk** - peaks computed once (at import, or on first open) and cached alongside the project (stems[].waveformCache in the .json), not recalculated on every open. Reason: performance on weak devices is a project requirement. Cache invalidated if the stem is re-imported.","x":1180,"y":700,"width":650,"height":380},
		{"id":"d1e2c3b4a5f60004","type":"text","text":"## IPC / Angular Decisions\n\n- **Commands queue in the Rust core** (not in Angular) - Angular only presents state and sends user intent (fires the Command right away, with no queue of its own); all the intelligence of queuing/ordering lives in Rust, to minimize a future framework swap. Per button/action: the control stays disabled until that Command's response arrives (prevents a double click) - this is just the UI reflecting a pending response. Between different actions: other buttons fire their own Commands without waiting; the core processes them in sequence. The user shouldn't notice the queue (it should drain fast); the UI doesn't apply an optimistic change, it only reflects state after the core confirms it.\n- **Errors as a modal** - audio_error and score_parse_error show up as a modal (not a toast, not a persistent banner). The modal is only about presentation: it doesn't halt the core. score_parse_error still doesn't halt the rest of the app (dismiss the modal, ChordView sits in an error state, the rest of the screen keeps working normally).","x":1880,"y":700,"width":650,"height":420}
	],
